---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import NormalizationReference from '../../components/NormalizationReference.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Database Normalization: 1NF, 2NF, 3NF Explained" description="A plain-English guide to 1NF, 2NF, 3NF, and BCNF with concrete examples.">
  <NormalizationReference client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>1NF, 2NF, 3NF: A Worked Example</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Form</th><th>Rule</th><th>Fixes</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1NF</td><td>Each column holds atomic values, no repeating groups</td><td>Splitting a "phone1, phone2" column into separate rows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2NF</td><td>1NF + no partial dependency on part of a composite key</td><td>Moving a product's price out of an order-line table into a products table</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3NF</td><td>2NF + no transitive dependency on non-key columns</td><td>Moving a customer's city (dependent on zip code, not the order) into a separate table</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Each form builds on the one before it — a table can't be in 3NF without also satisfying 1NF and 2NF.</p>

  <h2>Why 3NF is usually "enough" in practice</h2>
  <p>Full normalization to BCNF or beyond is academically interesting but rarely pursued in production schemas — most real-world databases target 3NF and then deliberately denormalize specific tables where read performance matters more than eliminating every last redundancy.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check your schema against each normal form's rule in order.</li>
    <li>Stop at the level of normalization appropriate for your actual performance and consistency needs.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Normalization Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chasing full normalization dogmatically</strong>, creating excessive joins that hurt query performance without a corresponding practical benefit.</li>
    <li><strong>Not recognizing partial dependencies</strong> (2NF violations) in composite-key tables, leading to update anomalies where the same fact needs updating in multiple rows.</li>
    <li><strong>Denormalizing without understanding what you're trading away</strong> — intentional denormalization for performance should be a conscious decision, not an accident of not understanding normal forms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Normalization Reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reviewing a schema design for redundancy and update anomaly risks</li>
    <li>Learning normalization concepts for a database design course or interview</li>
    <li>Deciding whether a proposed denormalization is a reasonable tradeoff</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Database Normalization FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What is the difference between 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF?</strong> 1NF requires atomic column values with no repeating groups. 2NF additionally requires no partial dependency on part of a composite key. 3NF additionally requires no transitive dependency between non-key columns.</p>
  <p><strong>What is an example of 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF?</strong> See the worked example table above — it walks through the same schema being progressively fixed at each normal form, showing exactly what changes and why.</p>
  <p><strong>Should every table be in 3NF?</strong> It's a solid general target, but some tables (especially read-heavy reporting tables) are intentionally denormalized for performance — normalization is a tool, not a strict requirement everywhere.</p>
</ToolLayout>
